<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohawk River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> River is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State that flows east for about 240 kilometres to join the Hudson north of Albany. Its valley, the homeland of the Mohawk nation, was the only low-level route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachians</a> and a main path of trade and war between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> and the Atlantic coast. In the 19th century it carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a>, which made New York City the leading port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohawk-river.jpg" alt="Mohawk River" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beyond My Ken · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2020_Cohoes_Falls_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> is the largest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a>. It begins where the West Branch and East Branch meet in north-central Oneida County, flows south past the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where it enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a>, and then runs east through the Mohawk Valley past Utica, Little Falls, Canajoharie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and Schenectady. It joins the Hudson at Cohoes, a few miles north of Albany. Its largest tributary, Schoharie Creek, supplies over a quarter of its watershed, and West Canada Creek adds about 16 per cent.</p><p>Around 13,350 years ago, in the last stage of the retreat of the ice, the catastrophic draining of the glacial Lake Iroquois passed through what became the Mohawk Valley. The torrent cut deep scour features such as the Potholes at Little Falls and laid down the sand and gravel of the Scotia Delta, also called the Great Flats Aquifer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river is named for the Mohawk Nation of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy. In the 1700s and before, the Mohawk were based in the valley, and their name for the river is Teionontatátie, a river flowing through a mountain. It served as their highway, and it is a passage through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a> between the Catskill Mountains to the south and the Adirondack Mountains to the north, easier than crossing the mountains. This made it strategically important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, and several important battles were fought there.</p><p>The Dutchman Harman Meyndertsz van den Bogaert made the first recorded European exploration in 1634, going 100 miles upstream from Albany. Dutch colonists founded Schenectady in 1661. In 1712 the British built Fort Hunter at the mouth of Schoharie Creek and settled about 600 Palatine Germans along the river. In the early nineteenth century a corporation built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> to Lake Erie, which eased westward settlement and linked the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> at Buffalo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Spring snowmelt brings the river's highest flows, and ice jams of some significance occur about every other year. The most severe flood on record was the spring breakup flood of 27 and 28 March 1914. The lower part of the river has five permanent dams, nine seasonal movable dams and five hydropower plants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mohawk River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Benedict Arnold, then fighting for the Continental Army, convinced the British by a ruse that a much larger force was arriving, and Brigadier General Barry St Leger abandoned the siege of Fort Stanwix in the <b>Mohawk River</b> Valley.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mohawk lived in the <b>Mohawk River</b> valley of eastern New York, the easternmost of the five Haudenosaunee nations.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
